667	#[derive(Default)]
668	struct State {
669	    /// Conversions by (type handle, depth), each entry with the enclosing instantiations it depended on. The handles
670	    /// are valid inside this snapshot only.
671	    ///
672	    /// Why an entry may be reused where [`Entry::holds_in`] says. A conversion reads its context in two ways only: the
673	    /// depth, which is in the key, and [`Cx::encloses`], the question whether a handle is an instantiation being
674	    /// expanded around the type. Everything else it reads is the checker's answer about the type, which does not change
675	    /// inside a snapshot. So a conversion is a deterministic walk whose every step is fixed by the type, the depth and
676	    /// the answers to those questions, in the order they are asked. Run it again where each handle it asked about has
677	    /// the same answer, and it asks the same questions in the same order and returns the same type. A handle that the
678	    /// conversion opened itself is settled inside it (it was found not to enclose the type at its own gate, which is
679	    /// recorded), so only the handles asked of the outer context are kept. A type in no cycle with its context asks
680	    /// nothing that encloses it and holds everywhere; one in a cycle holds where the same part of the cycle encloses
681	    /// it. Entries that hold in a context all agree, so which of them is found first, and so the order in which
682	    /// types were first reached, cannot show in the output.
683	    memo: Memo,
684	    origins: Mutex<FxHashMap<(bool, CompactSymbol), Option<Origin>>>,
685	    /// By (type handle, whether printed expanded: see [`Rpc::type_to_string_expanded`]).
686	    printed: Mutex<FxHashMap<(u64, bool), String>>,
687	    /// The parts of a type by its handle (see [`Parts`]). They are the checker's answer about the type, fixed inside the
688	    /// snapshot, while the memo converts a type again at another depth or under other instantiations: kept, that
689	    /// conversion asks for none of them again. Kept for the whole snapshot, since components of one library share
690	    /// most of their types: kept per component, a program of every Chakra component answered 16 at a time held 16
691	    /// copies of the same style props' members.
692	    parts: PartsSeen,
693	    /// Where props are declared ([`crate::declared`]), kept for the snapshot like the rest.
694	    declared: crate::declared::Memo,
695	    /// A map's property count by [`MapKey`]: a map without `as` has one property per key of its constraint and a cut reads
696	    /// only that count, so a [`settled_count`] list stands for every map of its key (docs/type-checker-integration.md,
697	    /// "Condition maps", says when that is exact). The handles are valid inside this snapshot only.
698	    maps: Mutex<FxHashMap<MapKey, usize>>,
699	    /// The props answered so far ([`crate::component::props_of_type`]), by what makes one ([`PropKey`]).
700	    props: KeptProps,
701	    /// The type of each symbol asked about so far, by its reference: what `getTypeOfSymbol` answers for a symbol does
702	    /// not change inside a snapshot. Chakra's props types are each their own instantiation, so each lists its own
703	    /// property symbols, but six in ten of those were listed by a component before (the same style props reached
704	    /// through the same instantiation), and their types need not be asked again.
705	    symbol_types: Mutex<SymbolTypes>,
706	    /// What the memo's entries hold, shared with the other lanes of the session ([`Converter::lanes`]).
707	    trees: Arc<Trees>,
708	    hits: AtomicU64,
709	    misses: AtomicU64,
710	}
